 Phoenix's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Phoenix has set <strong class=\"sig\">no standing record lows at all</strong>, far fewer than the 75 that chance alone would give, and <strong class=\"sig\">211 record highs</strong>, far more than the 75 that chance alone would give. The 2020s hold the most record highs of any decade (116). Phoenix's all-time high is 122\u00b0F, on June 26, 1990.","t_chg":"Phoenix's days and nights both warmed. Nights warmed faster.","p_chg":"Across 130 complete years: Phoenix's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.87\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.79 to +0.95), about +11.2\u00b0F since 1896. Phoenix's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.37\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.32 to +0.43), about +4.8\u00b0F since 1896.","t_mon":"Lows rose in all 12 months. Highs rose in all 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Phoenix's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.09 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 7.2 inches a year. The wettest year was 1905 (19.73 in); the driest, 1956 (2.82 in). Phoenix almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.0 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 26 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}